topics.recipes:
Start with a working skeleton: call start_from with no arguments to list templates, then start_from(template="rolling-course") to get a complete document. Edit the counts, dimensions, colours, names or rules to make it a different thing. This is the fastest way to a good result. Use apply=true to send a template through apply_text when an editor page is connected.

Recipes are model-written groups saved across server sessions. Save a group once you have built it twice: recipes(action="save", name="crate_stack", text="group crate_stack ... end", about="two crates stacked"). A recipe is one group named exactly like the recipe; expose the values you want to vary. list shows exposed parameter names, show returns the source and a call line to paste into your program, and delete removes it. Paste the shown group source ahead of the program that calls it, like a kit.

Use run for multi-step work and return only the useful result. Build, check, and read the delta in one call:
  const built = await tools.apply_text({text: program});
  const checked = await tools.check({});
  return {built, checked};

Loop over positions, applying one pipe-delimited row at each point:
  for (const [i, p] of positions.entries()) {
    await tools.apply_text({text: "piece_" + i + " = solid.box(size=[0.2, 0.2, 0.2]) | xform.move(by=[" + p.join(", ") + "]) | output piece_" + i});
  }
  return await tools.scene_overview({});

Apply edit operations, read the delta with diff, and return only changed lines:
  const edited = await tools.edit({ops: [{op: "set", node: "wall", params: {size: [4, 2, 0.2]}}]});
  const changed = await tools.diff({since: edited.revision - 1});
  return changed.lines;

topics.measure_reference:
measure

Measures the evaluated parts on screen, through the same geometry the viewer shows. By default a short text report per part: triangles, size, volume, watertight, thickness and the first problem with its fix. detail "full" gives the complete JSON report (topology, components, bounding shapes, symmetry, UVs). part names one output/part (default every part); checks (thickness, symmetry, uv, collision) limits which optional sections the full report keeps. A part that is not closed says where and what to do.

Parameters:
part: {"type":"string"}
detail: {"type":"string","enum":["summary","full"]}
checks: {"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["thickness","symmetry","uv","collision"]}}

topics.apply_text_reference:
apply_text

Adds the nodes described by a program in the text language to the document, evaluates it, and reports the outcome or the diagnostics (each with a line and column). Atomic: nothing changes unless all of it is valid. Limits: 256 KiB source text; translated batches allow 1,000 typed commands and 1 MiB. Every answer carries a `delta` (triangles and parts before and after, diagnostics, budget status, a one-line summary and a suggested next step), so you never need a second call to learn whether the edit worked. Pass dry_run=true to price and check a program without changing the document: it is applied, measured and undone, and the answer says would_apply and the same delta.

Parameters:
text: {"type":"string"}
label: {"type":"string"}
dry_run: true: apply, measure and undo; the document ends unchanged.

topics.playtest_reference:
playtest

Plays the document's game headless and reports what happened: status (won, lost or still playing), steps (60 per second of game time), final variables, how often each rule fired and which never did (never_fired: rule indexes that no play reached; a rule that cannot fire is usually a wrong selector or an unreachable goal). policy: "idle" (does nothing), "random" (seeded random input), or "seek:<hero tag>:<target tag>" (walks the hero (or, when the hero follows the viewer, the viewer at 3 m/s) to the nearest entity with the target tag, for example seek:hero:pickup; list several tags in order, seek:hero:key,gate,exit, to go for the first tag that still has a live entity). The same game, seed and policy give the same report every time. A game no policy can win is a design problem to fix with game.* nodes.

Parameters:
policy: {"type":"string"}
seed: {"type":"number"}
steps: Most steps to play, default 3600 (one minute), at most 36000.

topics.character_reference:
make_character

Adds a whole animated character or creature to the document: one smooth body skinned to a skeleton, with walk (main clip), run and idle clips, toon shaded with outlines. Give a body `plan` ("biped", "quadruped" or "serpent") with optional `sliders` (size in metres 0.2 to 20; head, limbs, torso, legs, neck, tail as multipliers 0.5 to 2; color [r,g,b] 0 to 1; horns and wings for a quadruped), or a whole `creature` you write yourself: bones (name, parent, head, tail, radius [at head, at tail], role leg|arm|spine|neck|head|tail|wing|free, pair for left/right copies defined on +x, phase) and blobs (name, center, radii). The creature stands on y=0 and faces +z. A `name` keeps several characters apart. Errors say which bone or slider to change. Call frame_view afterwards.

Parameters:
plan: {"type":"string","enum":["biped","quadruped","serpent"]}
sliders: {"type":"object"}
creature: {"type":"object"}
name: {"type":"string"}
label: {"type":"string"}

topics.command_reference:
apply_commands

Applies typed commands, for example [{"set_param":{"node":"n_abc12345","param":"segments","value":96}}]. Commands: add_node, remove_node, connect, disconnect, set_param, set_label, set_flags, set_output, and for groups set_instance_param, clear_instance_param, upgrade_group, expose_param, unexpose_param, set_group_output. A node may be named by its label instead of its id (the label of an existing node; ambiguous labels are refused with the ids they could mean). Atomic. Limit: 1,000 commands and 1 MiB per transaction. Answers carry a `delta` like apply_text; dry_run=true measures the commands and undoes them.

Parameters:
commands: {"type":"array","items":{"type":"object"}}
label: {"type":"string"}
dry_run: true: apply, measure and undo; the document ends unchanged.
